---
title: Features
nav_title: Features
description: A track-up radar, an alarm that gives the direction, ADS-L broadcast, traffic in your navigation app, six dials. Everything skyBlip Go does.
---

<header class="page-hero">
  <h1>Everything skyBlip Go does.</h1>
  <p class="page-hero-subtitle">
    Radar, alarm, ADS-L broadcast, Bluetooth, instruments.<br>
    In a pocket device, with no subscription.
  </p>
</header>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">The traffic</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="radar">A radar <span class="highlight">track up</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        You are in the centre, track up, and the sky turns around you. Traffic
        at the upper right is off the right of the nose, nothing to convert.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each aircraft is a shape: point up, it is above. Point down, below.
        Diamond, level with you. A chevron is added if it is climbing or
        descending, and a line leads to where it will be in a minute.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "radar", label: "Reading the radar", anchor: "heading-up" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Radar and traffic&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "radar.png", alt: "The radar page: three aircraft, one above, one below, one level and climbing, each with its one-minute line, and your track dotted" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points points--center">
    <li>Track up, never north</li>
    <li>Ring of 1, 2, 4 or 8&nbsp;NM</li>
    <li>Above, level, below</li>
    <li>A minute of track ahead of each aircraft</li>
    <li>The number of aircraft received</li>
    <li>Flight time, for the logbook</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="every-aircraft">Every aircraft, <span class="highlight">however far</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        With <span class="mono">PLOT ALL</span>, the factory setting, the ring
        leaves a band around it. An aircraft beyond the ring is placed on it,
        on its bearing, with its shape. Nothing received with a position is
        left out.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <span class="mono">PLOT TO SCALE</span>, the picture is to the
        scale of the ring, and stops at the edge of the screen.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "displays", label: "The radar, both ways", anchor: "both-ways" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Every screen&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "far-all.png", alt: "The radar page in PLOT ALL: two aircraft inside the ring, and on the band around it one aircraft ahead, a group of three at the upper right and one aircraft to the left" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="alarm">An alarm <span class="highlight">that says where to look</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Within 3&nbsp;km and 300&nbsp;m, a quarter of the screen centred on the
        traffic flashes, once a second, and the radar takes over whatever page is
        showing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The sector is tilted to the exact angle of the traffic. The device
        never tells you to climb or descend: it tells you to look.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "radar", label: "The alarm in detail", anchor: "alarm" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Radar and traffic&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "alarm.png", alt: "The radar page during an alarm: a 90° sector tilted towards the traffic, turned black" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points points--center">
    <li>3&nbsp;km around, 300&nbsp;m above and below</li>
    <li>A quarter of the screen, centred on the traffic</li>
    <li>One flash a second</li>
    <li>One touch on the pad silences it</li>
    <li>The radar takes over</li>
    <li>A warning, never an order</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="nearby">Who is there, <span class="highlight">nearest first</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        One line per aircraft: the source, ADS-L or FLARM, the callsign or
        address, the ground distance and the relative altitude.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "radar", label: "The Nearby list", anchor: "nearby" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Radar and traffic&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "nearby.png", alt: "The Nearby page: five aircraft nearest first, with their source, their callsign, their distance and their relative altitude" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/cta" %>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Being seen</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="broadcast">Your position, <span class="highlight">every second</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        In flight, it broadcasts your position in ADS-L once a second, on the
        ground once every ten seconds. The transmission lasts about 5&nbsp;ms,
        at a moment drawn from its address and the current second.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the rest of the second, it listens: both 868&nbsp;MHz channels for
        ADS-L and FLARM, and 869.525&nbsp;MHz, reserved for the uplink of future skyPost
        stations.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "radio", label: "Where the second goes", anchor: "second" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;The radio second&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "radio-log.png", alt: "The radio log: one line per frame received or sent, with its time, its channel, its address and its level" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points points--center">
    <li>ADS-L, the open standard from EASA</li>
    <li>1&nbsp;Hz in flight, 0.1&nbsp;Hz on the ground</li>
    <li>Locked to GPS to the microsecond</li>
    <li>ADS-L and FLARM reception</li>
    <li>Listening for future ground stations</li>
    <li>Half the permitted transmit time</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="callsign">Your callsign <span class="highlight">on other screens</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Typed on the device with two contacts, sent from your phone or set from
        the browser. It goes on air once every ten seconds, and shows on the
        screens of the aircraft around you.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "controls", label: "Typing your callsign", anchor: "callsign" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Controls and menus&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "menu-callsign.png", alt: "The callsign field: S-BLIP in large letters, a bar under the current character" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/cta" %>
</section>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">With your phone</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="apps">Three apps, <span class="highlight">no pairing</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        It shows up under its name, skyBlip Go followed by its address. The
        FLARM NMEA feed goes to SDVFR Next, SkyDemon, ForeFlight and the
        others: the tablet, the phone and the passenger&rsquo;s iPad see the
        same traffic.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its sixteen most recent flights download on the ground over the same
        link.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "phone", label: "With your phone", anchor: "by-name" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Phone and firmware&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot shot--photo">
      <%= image_tag "pages/index/skyblip-go-techo.avif", alt: "skyBlip Go, a white case with its antenna, the traffic radar on the screen" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points points--center">
    <li>No pairing, no code, no account</li>
    <li>Three connections at once</li>
    <li>SDVFR Next, SkyDemon, ForeFlight</li>
    <li>Its sixteen most recent flights in memory</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="asked">Every change <span class="highlight">asked on the screen</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        New firmware, settings, erasing your flights: the device does not take
        the phone at its word. It asks on its screen. Two presses allow, one
        refuses, silence counts as a refusal.
      </p>
      <p>
        Updates go through the browser, over Bluetooth. If the new version does
        not start, the device goes back to the old one.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "manage", label: "Manage my skyBlip" %>
        <small>Updates and settings, from Chrome or Edge</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "confirm.png", alt: "The authorisation prompt: AUTHORISE SETTINGS, PRESS TWICE TO ALLOW, ONE PRESS REFUSES" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/cta" %>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Day to day</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="instruments">Six dials <span class="highlight">off the GNSS</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Ground speed, horizon, altimeter, turn, track and vertical speed, with
        the battery above the middle dial.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of it is certified: it is not a horizon for flying in cloud.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "instruments", label: "The instruments", anchor: "six-pack" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Instruments&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "sixpack.png", alt: "The six-dial page: ground speed, horizon, altimeter, slip ball, track and vertical speed" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="controls">Two contacts, <span class="highlight">no phone</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        A tap on the pad changes page, a one-second touch brings back the
        radar. The button opens the menu: aircraft type, callsign, units,
        range, plot, alarm and volume.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "controls", label: "The controls", anchor: "two-contacts" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Controls and menus&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "menu-radar.png", alt: "The radar menu: aircraft type, callsign, units, range, plot, alarm and volume" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="power">A battery <span class="highlight">that warns you</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Without a position, it tells you how far the receiver has got. Switched
        off, the e-paper screen keeps the logo without power. On an empty
        battery, it says so on the screen before it cuts out.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-guide">
        <%= link_to_page "power", label: "The battery", anchor: "waiting" %>
        <small>From the guide &ldquo;Power and battery&rdquo;</small>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "off-flat-battery.png", alt: "The screen of a device switched off on an empty battery: the skyBlip logo and FLAT BATTERY" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points points--center">
    <li>850&nbsp;mAh battery</li>
    <li>Charges over USB-C</li>
    <li>200&times;200 e-paper screen</li>
    <li>BME280 barometer</li>
  </ul>

  <%= render "shared/cta" %>
</section>
